Falsifies `paging::x86_64::a_table_entry_is_never_a_block`.

The defect: `PS` set on a table pointer. Bit 7 is the page-size bit, and it is genuinely easy to
set here by accident because this encoder is already deliberately maximally permissive (`RW` and
`US` on every intermediate entry, since x86 ANDs the levels together), so one more bit in the
same `|` chain does not look out of place. It is not a permission: with `PS` set, a PDPTE maps a
1 GiB page and a PDE maps a 2 MiB page, at the physical address that was meant to be the next
table's. The walk stops early and the process is handed the page tables themselves.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!Ia32e::is_block(Ia32e::table_entry(pa)))`, for every `pa`, since
`is_block` here is `entry & PS != 0`.

The `Vtd` format next door encodes its own tables separately and is unaffected, which is why
this record names the `Ia32e` harness only.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
@@ -133,7 +133,7 @@
     fn table_entry(pa: u64, _level: usize) -> u64 {
         // Maximally permissive, because x86 ANDs the levels together and the leaf must be able to
         // grant anything. See notes/paging.md for what that trades away.
-        (pa & ADDR_MASK) | P | RW | US
+        (pa & ADDR_MASK) | P | RW | US | PS
     }
 
     fn leaf_entry(pa: u64, flags: Flags) -> u64 {
